  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Foreign in Two Homelands is showing an early but real scholarly footprint: it has 8 citations in OpenAlex, with recent uptake concentrated in migration, diaspora, and German history research. The book is also appearing in teaching contexts, including a recorded book talk, and it is available in open access form, which may support wider reading and classroom use.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Michelle Lynn Kahn\u2019s <em>Foreign in Two Homelands</em> examines the transnational history of Turkish migrants in Germany and the pressures of belonging across two societies that marked them as outsiders. Published by Cambridge University Press in the <em>Publications of the German Historical Institute</em> series, the book sits at the intersection of history and political science and is already being taken up by scholars working on migration, transnational identity, integration, and German memory culture.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is modest but meaningful for a 2024 book: OpenAlex shows 8 citing works, with most appearing in 2025 and 2026. The citing literature is concentrated in relevant humanities and social-science venues, including <em>The History of the Family</em>, <em>New German Critique</em>, <em>Rethinking History</em>, and <em>Population Space and Place</em>. That pattern suggests the book is beginning to shape conversations across adjacent fields rather than remaining confined to a single subdiscipline.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Its influence also appears in the kinds of topics the citing works address. Recent scholarship linked to the book focuses on Turkish families in Europe, postmigrant belonging, discrimination across generations, and German memory politics. This is consistent with a work that is being used not simply as a reference point, but as a framing text for broader discussions of migration history, identity, and state responses to mobility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are also signs of pedagogical reach. A Fresno State History Department book talk features the author discussing the volume, and library holdings indicate discoverability in institutional collections. In addition, the record shows open access availability through an open repository instance, which may help the book circulate beyond subscription channels. Public discourse evidence is sparse in the archive, so the clearest picture comes from scholarship and teaching rather than from media attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, <em>Foreign in Two Homelands</em> is an emerging scholarly anchor on Turkish migration in Germany: early citations are accumulating in the right research communities, and the book is already visible in teaching and open-access pathways. The record points to a work with growing disciplinary relevance and clear potential for broader uptake as the literature develops.</p>",
